You could write your own template filter for this. Create a new python file called 'grade_filters.py' in a folder called 'templatetags' in your project root folder and declare a new templatetag as follows:

    from django import template

    register = template.Library()

    grade_dict = {
        'A+': 10, 
        'A':9, 
        'A-':8
    }

Some critical details are lacking from your post, so this is all speculation, but depending on how you have your model and view implemented, you could do this in several ways.  
If you have all your grades for a student together, you could pass it a simple list and sum the results  

     @register.filter(name='sum_of_grades')
     def return_item(grade_list):
        new_grades_list = [grade_dict.get(grade, 0) for grade in grade_list]
        try:
            return sum(new_grades_list)
        except:
            return 0

Or you could do something similar by passing a user to the filter if they have a foreign key relation.

    @register.filter(name='sum_of_grades')
    def return_item(user):
        new_grades_list = [grade_dict.get(grade, 0) for grade in user.grade_set.all()]
        try:
            return sum(new_grades_list)
        except:
            return 0

You can even chain tags together if necessary

    @register.filter(name='get_list_for_student')
    def return_item(student_list, name):

        return student_list.filter(name=name)

Once you have created these tags, be sure to restart your development server for the new tag to be recognized: https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/1.6/howto/custom-template-tags/#code-layout

Now, in your template you can load the new templatetag with

    {% load grade_filters %}



For each of the examples above you can calculate the grade total of one student with, respectively:

    {{ list_of_grades|sum_of_grades }}
    {{ user|sum_of_grades }}
Or to combine multiple tags(this is probably unnecessary, but it demonstrates a point)

    {{ gradelist|get_list_for_student:'John'|sum_of_grades }}

Of course there are other ways, but without more details it is hard to offer anything concrete.

ph_text:
You could write your own template filter for this. Create a new python file called 'grade_filters.py' in a folder called 'templatetags' in your template folder and declare a new templatetag as follows:

    from django import template

    register = template.Library()

    def grade_to_number(grade):
        map_dict = {'A+': 10, 'A':9, 'A-':8}
        if grade in map_dict:
            return map_dict[grade]
        else:
            return 0


    @register.filter(name='sum_of_grades')
    def return_item(grade_list):

        new_grades_list = [grade_to_number(grade) for grade in grade_list]
        try:
            return sum(new_grades_list)
        except:
            return 0

    @register.filter(name='get_list_for_student')
    def return_item(student_list, name):

        return student_list.filter(name=name)

Now, in your template you can load the new templatetag with

    {% load grade_filters %}

Calculate the grade total of one student with:

    {{ gradelist|get_list_for_student:'John'|sum_of_grades }}

