    class RotateMat {
        static int[][] transpose(int[][] arr) {
            assert arr.length == arr[0].length;
            int m = arr.length;
            for (int i=0; i<m;i++) {
                for (int k=0; k<m;k++) {
                    int temp = arr[i][k];
                    arr[i][k] = arr[k][i];
                    arr[k][i] = temp;
                }
            }
            print(arr);
            return arr;
        }

        static void print(int[][] arr) {
            int n=arr[0].length;
            int m=arr.length;
            assert m==n;
            //System.out.println(m  + " " + n);

            for (int i=0; i<m;i++) {
                 for (int j=0; j<n; j++) {
                     System.out.print(arr[i][j]);
                 }
                 System.out.println();
            }
        }

        public static void main(String[] args) {
            int arr[][] = { {1,2,3}, {4,5,6}, {7,8,9}};
            print(arr);
            arr = transpose(arr);
            print(arr);
        }
    }

I am a C user and trying to practice more java programming. I understand pass by value in java and that is the reason I try to return the transposed matrix assign it again to `arr` variable. 

I get the same array printed even after I transpose....What am I doing wrong here?

**Update:**

Because I used `for (int k=0; k<m;k++)`, every element was transposed twice: once as `arr[i][k]`, and once as `arr[k][i]`.  This means it was transposed back to its original location.

