Local arrays within a kernel, as the one you have defined are allocated in registers and in local memory when there are not enough register.

If you want allocate the array in shared memory you must specify it as follow:

    __shared__ int my_array[100];

Sug_Text:
`l` and `r` stand for "left" and "right", respectively.

The segmentation fault is happening because you're passing `l = 10`, so `while (a[++i] < v);` breaks.

**[edit]**

    while (a[++i] < v);                                
    while (a[--j] > v);

These two loops are also problematic: you need to test that `i` and `j` are not out of bounds.

v is actually  the rightmost element, so it is the sentinel for the first loop. 
The second loop needs a check of j when it reaches the leftmost cell. e.g. l ('el') so the
condition a[--l] doesn't occur when l=0. This is in Sedgewick. I thought is was 1 ('one'), not l, and wondered why I had a 'mostly' sorted array.

Sedgewick uses the preorder increment and decrement to keep the scan going, so he starts with i = l -1 ,and j = r , because at r is the pivot. 
When trying to figure out partition, a lot of people make i=l and j=r-1 and test before doing any scanning, so they have to do the swap and remember to increment and decrement after the swap, otherwise the recently swapped cells can get rescanned, and this causes problems when , I think the second scan can't go on because it is j=l e.g.


>int i,j; i = l; j = r -1; 

>for(;;) {

>  while ( i < v) ++i;

>  while ( v < j ) { 

      if (j==l) break;
      --j;
>   }

>   if (i>=j) break;

>   swap( a, i++, j--);

>}

You can easily achieve this using modulus division to compute the remainder: 

    def round_down(num, divisor):
        return num - (num % divisor)

