More elegant way would be using a regular expression ( regex ) as follows:


    public class DecimalDigitsInputFilter implements InputFilter {

        Pattern mPattern;

        public DecimalDigitsInputFilter(int digitsBeforeZero,int digitsAfterZero) {
                mPattern=Pattern.compile("[0-9]{0," + (digitsBeforeZero-1) + "}+((\\.[0-9]{0," + (digitsAfterZero-1) + "})?)||(\\.)?");
        }

        @Override
        public CharSequence filter(CharSequence source,        int start, int end,        Spanned dest, int dstart, int dend) {

                        String string = new StringBuilder(dest)
                        .replace(dstart, dend, //replace the portion of dest from dstart to dend
                                        new StringBuilder(source) //with a substring of source
                        .substring(start, end)).toString(); //from start to end

                        Matcher matcher = mPattern.matcher(string);

                        if(!matcher.matches())
                                return "";
                        return null;
            }

    }

To use it do: 

    editText.setFilters(new InputFilter[] {new DecimalDigitsInputFilter(5,2)});

http://developer.android.com/reference/android/text/InputFilter.html

